nswer-field/id #uuid "d9a64153-0821-5729-8176-a4052e15c45b" :answer-field/key "The point $x=-1$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468324} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3af30520-887d-57b9-b763-1a4ee2280a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668599} {:answer/id #uuid "f134915e-c9cf-5c7a-a15e-7a6db9562418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3af30520-887d-57b9-b763-1a4ee2280a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668599} :answer-field/id #uuid "c104466e-d54b-5676-8e91-3cf65f569dec" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d598b560-2b94-5f0a-b115-9618b72a3e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-342303" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, statement “The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ are analytic about $x=-1.$” is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1,$ multiplying them by $(x+1)$ and $(x+1)^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=-1.$ Therefore, statement “The functions $(x+1)P(x)$ and $(x+1)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1.$” is true.\n\n- Since statement “The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ are analytic about $x=-1.$” is true, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement “The point $x=-1$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation” is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ are analytic about $x=-1.$”; “The functions $(x+1)P(x)$ and $(x+1)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1.$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "678f909e-0e86-5a06-9fd5-059fd523e869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467208} {:answer/id #uuid "b07945a2-7778-5891-b7fb-60c7573312ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "678f909e-0e86-5a06-9fd5-059fd523e869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467208} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc4cd968-c209-5480-a88d-a3c1785fbe68" :answer-field/key "The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468325} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f864cfd-6310-595f-95e2-2589c7cb9991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467210} {:answer/id #uuid "c161f061-3c8c-5687-b3b3-6d1602c1dfac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f864cfd-6310-595f-95e2-2589c7cb9991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467210} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c7f96d3-cc42-5d74-91fb-d5adf8539cb3" :answer-field/key "The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468326} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8986e212-c639-5abf-b616-c3d6b4e1b244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467212} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5fa0b5-647d-500d-9ef8-ae8c45dc7293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a5fa0b5-647d-500d-9ef8-ae8c45dc7293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467213} :answer-field/id #uuid "bba3aa18-775b-5806-a452-96cfddbbd6dd" :answer-field/key "The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468327} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0b9eb0-437e-5af2-905f-85b172152c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668601} {:answer/id #uuid "af2f10da-69b0-582c-84a6-2f8e2621dbbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0b9eb0-437e-5af2-905f-85b172152c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668601} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab0e58ad-9815-51ba-aad7-1910fd05e218" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8575f381-396c-5d27-97ba-96e6bcb9f145" :question/math-academy-id "q-342307" :question/problem "$$\n(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement “The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$” is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\frac{1}{x+2}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ while the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement “The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$” is false.\n\n- Since statement “The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$” is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement “The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$” is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement “The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation” is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "718e262f-702d-56a3-8244-93f04bfef53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467214} {:answer/id #uuid "d33c017a-0f5d-5aa8-ba9c-c5d244f3eff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "718e262f-702d-56a3-8244-93f04bfef53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467214} :answer-field/id #uuid "959c5bdb-6293-590c-9b69-fe8ed74b9410" :answer-field/key "The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$" :answer-field/type