db/id #edb/ref 17592186668924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f47bb47-6168-5cb8-92db-b7a3fc493275" :question/math-academy-id "q-73106" :question/problem "![](images/89/89dca4b059ccbd1fe93d000acabcbd33b5fc10389de48ef3cbae65cb4455f29e.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “The function is left-continuous at $x=1$” is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 3=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is right-continuous at $x=1$” is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=1\\ne 3=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function is continuous at $x=1$” is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4ee9b9a-eb51-5e15-abb2-05bb1b86b139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468082} {:answer/id #uuid "f22d2656-8795-5d89-ac40-9e4cae3d15a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468083}] :answer-field/correct {:a