er-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470333} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8546fa73-dcec-50dc-974d-858421123cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469444} {:answer/id #uuid "0bbd1552-1f4e-52a4-a141-e1fb530579a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8546fa73-dcec-50dc-974d-858421123cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469444} :answer-field/id #uuid "00caafb1-473f-5b30-8fec-fff060993a90" :answer-field/key "$\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "817ecd59-8e82-5f2d-9ecb-8b5582faeb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668727} {:answer/id #uuid "bd147ff9-91f1-5381-8931-f0e9bb8c2202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "817ecd59-8e82-5f2d-9ecb-8b5582faeb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668727} :answer-field/id #uuid "2291b4ef-e385-501a-936c-7f8aa2bbd1f6" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b045a52-4215-5bdb-9dcf-5641984e3591" :question/math-academy-id "q-86898" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=|x|.$ If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/23/23f31619eda46f1e8604695c4056d4c0c5bb8ee1e186b1316151762d5b5c8e59.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$ and $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ equal $\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$; $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1dca5fa-a9a7-5936-992b-453ceb07007e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469536} {:answer/id #uuid "dcce086a-9e68-5c53-8e57-74901b19c09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1dca5fa-a9a7-5936-992b-453ceb07007e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469536} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d55eb78-31a4-5821-8b16-c576014f0f3f" :answer-field/key "$(1,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470380} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86e378b6-0af4-5188-a5e7-6414457f0464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469538} {:answer/id #uuid "82aee8a9-7717-5a7f-8a54-90dfa5eb69ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86e378b6-0af4-5188-a5e7-6414457f0464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469538} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e35b08f-b0c4-534c-9673-873356909333" :answer-field/key "$(2,-2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470381} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d6ac48f-d971-55cb-8b73-be946f446094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469540} {:answer/id #uuid "ad963729-b8c6-572d-b56a-b69a8f1b73af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d6ac48f-d971-55cb-8b73-be946f446094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469540} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cac8d36-0d1c-5fa4-a1fa-2903f72a8f08" :answer-field/key "$(e,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470382} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d680240-1d25-5843-87f4-ef89dcf7284f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668729} {:answer/id #uuid "69dd8594-8e27-56d1-af07-de4791aa95d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69dd8594-8e27-56d1-af07-de4791aa95d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668730} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5df78a-5f99-5a9a-8fd8-e29a243a8125" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e87b95d9-4b1d-5eb0-8c89-8d155a899eb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-89871" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,0)} & ={\\left(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}\\right)|}_{(1,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,-2)} & ={\\left(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}\\right)|}_{(2,-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{4} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(e,1)} & ={\\left(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}\\right)|}_{(e,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the points are the stationary points of the function.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d03fa37-ca1a-54f9-a3eb-5a8bf1effac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469548} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc192d1-a596-57f1-9e52-7df28aa3631a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d03fa37-ca1a-54f9-a3eb-5a8bf1effac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469548} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7d22a00-7d6c-5c4f-863f-4b796b7ee608" :answer-field/key "$(\\pi ,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470386} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85798085-cefc-5be0-8526-fcbe0d58d21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469550} {:answer/id #uuid "468e2470-d7f7-5d4e-92ac-43fe7a8570ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85798085-cefc-5be0-8526-fcbe0d58d21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469550} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b85f1f5-9e6e-5497-98df-d403ecceb824" :answer-field/key "$(\\pi ,1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb0b04ec-3384-54b7-bdeb-6b2b7ad2fe88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469552} {:answer/id #uuid "5188a600-1360-5a9d-9060-fbdd68ab3467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb0b04ec-3384-54b7-bdeb-6b2b7ad2fe88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469552} :answer-field/id #uuid "51d29dc4-cdf3-5410-965e-98b9294f009d" :answer-field/key "$(\\pi ,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470388} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acdab16b-82d5-51aa-a2d5-79fae210b5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668731} {:answer/id #uuid "0cbedbe3-3676-5e87-8482-ff0c5fa59989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cbedbe3-3676-5e87-8482-ff0c5fa59989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668732} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8b00336-c0c0-570d-b881-57b371691c99" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e244cae-49ac-5727-b109-5857ab506f84" :question/math-academy-id "q-89887" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\pi ,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+0-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$(\\pi ,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)}=1\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\pi ,1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement “$(\\pi ,1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$” is false. There is no critical point at $(\\pi ,1),$ therefore we cannot have a local maximum at that point.\n\n- Statement “$(\\pi ,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$” is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\pi ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7336966f-8336-50bb-9351-9f946f8a93c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469554} {:answer/id #uuid "2f19c276-4dd0-5818-a7e8-e0a8c790f637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f19c276-4dd0-5818-a7e8-e0a8c790f637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469555} :answer-field/id #uuid "52922013-663f-5486-a5b8-b73490734552" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470389} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67d3ff23-6cd5-5077-95e9-4bf353e699f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469556} {:answer/id #uuid "f7fbe06b-4f03-5ee5-8347-c57fabde826e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67d3ff23-6cd5-5077-95e9-4bf353e699f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469556} :answer-field/id #uuid "c58572a0-d4f3-5a9c-ae1f-3e90b3667207" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470390} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4704dc4d-5c8e-52aa-9ff6-c866e334a419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469558} {:answer/id #uuid "afd9e88a-8078-53b1-a2cf-af582cabf5e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4704dc4d-5c8e-52aa-9ff6-c866e334a419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469558} :answer-field/id #uuid "662a2990-175d-596c-894a-52061ccb6fc3" :answer-field/key "$(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8fc6cc-5f63-535a-a41c-20a089d2f902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668733} {:answer/id #uuid "f849c348-973b-519e-a9e5-7badc5bf3144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d8fc6cc-5f63-535a-a41c-20a089d2f902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668733} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e69e1d7-0768-5277-bed2-3fcf6981e370" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0272b49a-4d96-50b3-ac67-e22a2911e738" :question/math-academy-id "q-89888" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0+4-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement “$(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$” is false. There is a critical point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement “$(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$” is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c862053a-5bee-5c38-9662-6c23de46fd60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469560} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5d7089-7e96-5d4f-b54c-c78b0d049d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e5d7089-7e96-5d4f-b54c-c78b0d049d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469561} :answer-field/id #uuid "5276d74f-53e0-56a1-8026-c1dc297a84ec" :answer-field/key "$(0,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470392} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c6c0a2f-2737-5f31-a5e5-7fdf7d0d1363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469562} {:answer/id #uuid "a31f52be-8332-5208-8d5b-24d230d9e98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a31f52be-8332-5208-8d5b-24d230d9e98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469563} :answer-field/id #uuid "b17d2099-9152-56aa-9e12-26564c12b1e1" :answer-field/key "$(0,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470393} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d27c83d1-4a31-5df4-b39b-1772bafc76a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469564} {:answer/id #uuid "3d07a4d3-c170-5686-8fe2-c9bd16b273ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d27c83d1-4a31-5df4-b39b-1772bafc76a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469564} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d9d4167-bf75-5351-b7f0-fba4892eca44" :answer-field/key "$(0,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470394} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67866448-9440-5f1b-b598-38de425d7141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668735} {:answer/id #uuid "e2287095-63f2-5481-98dd-aff0e702f597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67866448-9440-5f1b-b598-38de425d7141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668735} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed188f66-be7e-5b8c-b0a5-186bd940cb6b" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7cbbdaa7-7656-5f0c-adc2-8162521aa896" :question/math-academy-id "q-89894" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(4xy)|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =4(0)(1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)\\cdot y+4x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =4y+4x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4y+4x(4xy) \\\\  & =4y(1+4{x}^{2}) \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(4y(1+4{x}^{2}))|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =4(1)(1+4(0)^{2}) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$(0,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement “$(0,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$” is true. There is a critical point at $(0,1).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)}>0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement “$(0,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$” is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$(0,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$”; “$(0,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1d9da7c-a072-5f3b-af33-b2866c1d2152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469566} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0d05c0-ca73-5a2e-985e-0892c66c1b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d0d05c0-ca73-5a2e-985e-0892c66c1b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469567} :answer-field/id #uuid "fabaf909-99d9-5a89-9004-1e924a5fdf93" :answer-field/key "$(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470395} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a0548f0-1a15-5b3f-a6f2-8bb3444061c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469568} {:answer/id #uuid "bf535b84-3986-5327-8aa0-eab77a130dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a0548f0-1a15-5b3f-a6f2-8bb3444061c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469568} :answer-field/id #uuid "587840e5-864f-5720-953f-25edd60903ed" :answer-field/key "$(0,-3)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ed10466-1946-5a24-8c37-950f6d68b65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469570} {:answer/id #uuid "a39ac4dc-3a76-5f75-aeb1-e452c3bb0a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ed10466-1946-5a24-8c37-950f6d68b65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469570} :answer-field/id #uuid "90377193-212f-54e9-849e-664163a14855" :answer-field/key "$(0,-3)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470397} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3426bd8d-a14e-50b7-9bb9-200dcd6afc5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668737} {:answer/id #uuid "444263c1-ec92-5886-87ae-94f5018ab68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3426bd8d-a14e-50b7-9bb9-200dcd6afc5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668737} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd76097d-2e40-5ca2-a6bf-8c3f34a0f8df" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e18f8cd9-b1f2-5b99-9f93-cbb0a6aa2a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-89895" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,-3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,-3),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-3)} & ={\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)|}_{(0,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{(-3)} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot y-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-x\\cdot \\frac{x}{y})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-3)} & ={\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}\\right)|}_{(0,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)^{2}-{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-3)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement “$(0,-3)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$” is false. There is a critical point at $(0,-3).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-3)}<0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local maximum (not a local minimum) at that point.\n\n- Statement “$(0,-3)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$” is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d647a060-72ca-57fb-a5fe-eff8314c12e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469572} {:answer/id #uuid "25e97890-ed76-54f5-ba7f-87f260ab450e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d647a060-72ca-57fb-a5fe-eff8314c12e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469572} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fcc0713-29f5-5278-903c-5e98e96e6154" :answer-field/key "$f(y)={y}^{2}+y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470398} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d4cfaa-6627-5d83-97bb-7b6641e1f3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469574} {:answer/id #uuid "b04306a9-85bc-550f-a326-d2233476b975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b04306a9-85bc-550f-a326-d2233476b975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469575} :answer-field/id #uuid "2814b6ad-5c31-5eec-8fa4-71775f9ab65a" :answer-field/key "$g(x,y)={x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470399} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d62f14b6-d71c-5928-9c50-515e215671c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469576} {:answer/id #uuid "7eae1f5b-bb9f-5bc6-84fd-d054c84e24af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eae1f5b-bb9f-5bc6-84fd-d054c84e24af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469577} :answer-field/id #uuid "d880d005-a681-5f07-bca8-dd8df28b76ac" :answer-field/key "$h(x,y,z)=xyz$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470400} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4f32f00-71d7-53aa-bb6d-c24b7177b575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668739} {:answer/id #uuid "3f962dbe-c54e-5a09-a7bd-23f62934fb62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4f32f00-71d7-53aa-bb6d-c24b7177b575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668739} :answer-field/id #uuid "5946e14c-5ba2-5b4b-bc4f-36ab81994cd0" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a2bd93d-e29f-5907-89bd-df4c7adaca45" :question/math-academy-id "q-90582" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(y)$  is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2.$ However, it also contains a term ($y$) that is of degree $1.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $2.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4.$ It is a polynomial of degree $4,$ and all of its terms are of degree $4.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ with no terms of degree other than $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0006d664-b8ff-5634-986f-5c0d4e1d7dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469884} {:answer/id #uuid "83c6dacd-fb1e-5471-803a-abd44bac5e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0006d664-b8ff-5634-986f-5c0d4e1d7dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469884} :answer-field/id #uuid "eebde2a3-3507-5638-9b44-f686205ba48d" :answer-field/key "At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a positive slope" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470554} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d127a6-6075-5768-b2dc-3a7b506eee92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469886} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbf38e8-b177-50e1-9fb8-9e14988ed9f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dbf38e8-b177-50e1-9fb8-9e14988ed9f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469887} :answer-field/id #uuid "318d7144-ff77-5eb1-a82b-9cbffe6faa92" :answer-field/key "At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470555} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179c1b11-36a5-5f98-920b-76f389dbee6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469888} {:answer/id #uuid "d13d24a9-2960-5539-8b04-298e83c2a041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "179c1b11-36a5-5f98-920b-76f389dbee6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469888} :answer-field/id #uuid "403154ae-9845-5bc7-b450-f3877f734072" :answer-field/key "At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a negative slope" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470556} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae051eee-82e6-54bc-b08b-ce9acde1b605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668741} {:answer/id #uuid "07baf03f-5b6e-5ebe-a9eb-0aaa6ab1f78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae051eee-82e6-54bc-b08b-ce9acde1b605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668741} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6690cf0-5b6e-5024-a534-fb2c8850d7dd" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cae22cc3-a67e-53bb-bb36-eebf3af162ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-97288" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a positive slope” is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-2+1=-1<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement “At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal” is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-1)}=-1+1=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement “At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope field has a negative slope” is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}=1+1=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,1)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “At every point along the line $y=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal” only.\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/caa69f6116eba6462c4fe18f2e535d83db350f0797cf1fcd895ac6df9a4d6fd7.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172039 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95bf5c87-5d3a-5cc2-9253-891ef7730177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438628} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5d5215-469a-5799-ba24-eb8bc4c358d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95bf5c87-5d3a-5cc2-9253-891ef7730177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438628} :answer-field/id #uuid "f545d324-f9c3-507e-983b-fd1001340ff1" :answer-field/key "At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b8f3b3-189f-5b75-9ccc-ddd834e75d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438630} {:answer/id #uuid "d83106e2-c95a-541f-8d33-b2ba054c50ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d83106e2-c95a-541f-8d33-b2ba054c50ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438631} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7dbe69a-f051-5886-8cde-e2c1753c8657" :answer-field/key "At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e25dae65-1d55-511a-b24e-a8cb350771d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438632} {:answer/id #uuid "41b3f2e3-0dfd-5365-982e-fd4103306c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e25dae65-1d55-511a-b24e-a8cb350771d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438632} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8887387-6555-5bd2-8c67-a09c271d9b0f" :answer-field/key "At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439345} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd1c2bdc-0469-523c-91f6-c855085c7da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668743} {:answer/id #uuid "51177707-2eb7-5aed-a139-336e31875353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd1c2bdc-0469-523c-91f6-c855085c7da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668743} :answer-field/id #uuid "df2afb4d-c42b-5d56-a228-5938eaf763e0" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ebbc31b-8f74-56d3-8d4f-392a8aeeda85" :question/math-academy-id "q-97477" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=-xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172041 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4842db83-277f-5ac6-9756-7d71ed57a360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438652} {:answer/id #uuid "aff73e0e-7657-51b7-a7f5-1be162f86243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4842db83-277f-5ac6-9756-7d71ed57a360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438652} :answer-field/id #uuid "3021006b-6d7f-5efe-b7a1-726f767d922d" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439355} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a32b2ab2-8963-54b9-9a32-d18a9a92c5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438654} {:answer/id #uuid "9b16c32e-6930-5573-ab6e-d3adf9fffe07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a32b2ab2-8963-54b9-9a32-d18a9a92c5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438654} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9933f56-9026-5d7c-b64d-f7f6c2a23d9e" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439356} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6c52061-cde6-58fd-9539-45c706276407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438656} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf6408d-db98-52dd-9aa1-9d5f8b43eba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcf6408d-db98-52dd-9aa1-9d5f8b43eba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438657} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab818855-7afa-5726-ad99-5b0158d162ae" :answer-field/key "At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y>1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439357} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f02c28-218d-5002-8dec-284e88dcbc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668745} {:answer/id #uuid "be2fad4c-0d0d-52a9-bda4-d5157c58f8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02f02c28-218d-5002-8dec-284e88dcbc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668745} :answer-field/id #uuid "24e2accc-de79-5b59-9349-8d3ce4e4465d" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ca0f65d-7276-53ff-adf8-200ee3f85dcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-97534" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x-1)(y-1)^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x-1)(y-1)^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=1$ or $y=1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/07/075264fa042adc64e160a83f03a2b125dd5a9b0927fc3ed318778c0004689ec4.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/57/5716d59a745fc148b504a4db523286d7c7209fe793fcf369a90d220125f7e9fb.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” is false. If $x<1$ and $y<1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y<1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $x>1$ and $y<1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y>1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $x>1$ and $y>1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "641505e3-4fec-567c-9c83-85531b7e7c70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469898} {:answer/id #uuid "8c339b98-1358-5e7d-8176-5121ba8d7389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "641505e3-4fec-567c-9c83-85531b7e7c70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469898} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8358553-9442-58c8-8202-498365ddb0e4" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+1)^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "522e362c-1cf2-5e5e-bf1e-ef974c5457f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469900} {:answer/id #uuid "daaa37b4-08b7-53d2-adb1-dcb759580aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "522e362c-1cf2-5e5e-bf1e-ef974c5457f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469900} :answer-field/id #uuid "18be5b77-e262-5137-851e-aadfcd89dc06" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470562} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c57acda6-1c6a-5883-9027-7b21a18b0906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469902} {:answer/id #uuid "0a5ae1cb-d891-5e47-9927-87731b1815d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a5ae1cb-d891-5e47-9927-87731b1815d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469903} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcd41de6-cafd-59b8-852b-7d4b0662b46c" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470563} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272dd19e-0e02-5ae9-8ada-ce095a27581e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668747} {:answer/id #uuid "d9651ec9-66a4-5e88-8075-4d530f2c0306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "272dd19e-0e02-5ae9-8ada-ce095a27581e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668747} :answer-field/id #uuid "86285223-260c-5d76-af14-99f4e970d98d" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4cdb362d-f774-5da6-bc15-c04006a3144a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98632" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca296c3a8a2989fd5e5ca647ad4f7d0f4890aa3caf8d9e0a1fce98498d2d7d64.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y+1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=-1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+1)^{2}$ does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1)$ does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-y-2.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y+1)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y-2$ has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y-2$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d8db14d-ee62-5f27-9e1d-94fabe34d298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469904} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fc96b7-4a76-5f62-a63c-72f8b35a31dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3fc96b7-4a76-5f62-a63c-72f8b35a31dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469905} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c7181d-3159-5eb8-8297-7334eac26868" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y({y}^{2}-4)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470564} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce722e7d-8ced-56bb-8b95-f9228089b504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469906} {:answer/id #uuid "51b5c6a0-f8a9-5df5-96b9-6b8e0cb79e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce722e7d-8ced-56bb-8b95-f9228089b504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469906} :answer-field/id #uuid "cedc6c80-dac3-5be1-b567-60fe9aa755cd" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y(4-{y}^{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "886dc1a9-31f9-5e08-a537-41e9c2a3e5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469908} {:answer/id #uuid "ba36d9ea-19f6-5bd9-8316-4a6de407a017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "886dc1a9-31f9-5e08-a537-41e9c2a3e5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469908} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fbef377-40b9-5ebf-8afc-8f580c32d99f" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470566} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6ecf719-ab16-5c8e-a0b4-0b6ea1db6081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668749} {:answer/id #uuid "d489644b-6db9-585f-b516-d0c2fc23c1e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6ecf719-ab16-5c8e-a0b4-0b6ea1db6081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668749} :answer-field/id #uuid "85082791-7827-5a40-b059-4be73ab55dc5" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98ee9b06-5212-535a-b3fd-5d1e48bb48f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-98636" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5afe944331145c5253b2f1100f642b8a91a538295f3c84877eeb86870a04915.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=y({y}^{2}-4).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=y(y+2)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (0,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-2,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y({y}^{2}-4)$ has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=y(4-{y}^{2}).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=y(4-{y}^{2}),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y(4-{y}^{2})$ does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)={y}^{2}-4,$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=\\pm 2$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-4$ does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y({y}^{2}-4)$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec7790f1-e74c-577a-9fac-f4ad0834e7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469916} {:answer/id #uuid "544703b8-aee8-5172-a235-fe2dd9d223f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec7790f1-e74c-577a-9fac-f4ad0834e7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469916} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d06c494-d9ab-58f8-8aa4-790cf3c7e1f5" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470570} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8fc17b6-9029-54fe-8151-860ee2a28ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469918} {:answer/id #uuid "f60f0f4a-cd20-50b1-86bc-47b454ff1491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60f0f4a-cd20-50b1-86bc-47b454ff1491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469919} :answer-field/id #uuid "01975edc-764b-5829-a3ff-c04974e897a0" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fc85124-4b3f-5452-8d24-ba7bad684c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469920} {:answer/id #uuid "f0710156-43ac-518c-bd75-c5d772369c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fc85124-4b3f-5452-8d24-ba7bad684c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469920} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21e23dc-b213-5dcc-ab9b-58c4fbcd44f2" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+2y$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470572} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "729b3f0e-dc1f-5be6-bf49-460f81549438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668751} {:answer/id #uuid "2be847cb-6492-5679-b8f9-cbc668953bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "729b3f0e-dc1f-5be6-bf49-460f81549438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668751} :answer-field/id #uuid "352dc6b0-9f8e-56c0-b923-ffb938f22d35" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77dc20d1-343d-5ade-92ad-7f182610538c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98643" :question/problem "![](images/96/96740fdcc0dc6aa22fa097ba714669fae4ef79543aafad72b3fc3db7722f83d1.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,0).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-y(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (0,2).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)$ does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=-y(y-2)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-y(y-2)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-y$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for  $y\\in (-\\infty ,0),$ and $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)^{2}$ has the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=y(y+2),$ and the equilibrium solution are $y=-2,0.$ \n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+2y$ does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y-2)^{2}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df839928-3f6f-55e0-8ce3-ec178f216622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469922} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6dbeeb-7472-57c7-bccd-8dabac257990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df839928-3f6f-55e0-8ce3-ec178f216622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469922} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee4327d-dc7a-5d44-8201-3e5c06fed984" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470573} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1a270f2-6e76-58ad-b29f-a44eb2c0ae8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469924} {:answer/id #uuid "625f9e50-ee73-5f92-a569-3766007bff23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1a270f2-6e76-58ad-b29f-a44eb2c0ae8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469924} :answer-field/id #uuid "44758402-cc1c-5952-aa33-e66b0505929c" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+2)(y-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470574} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67fab95f-3e9a-5af1-a273-39130055d0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469926} {:answer/id #uuid "4d49f84f-a402-5b92-826f-647f032b17bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d49f84f-a402-5b92-826f-647f032b17bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469927} :answer-field/id #uuid "88057ab1-b1cf-5762-bfd3-e9cb8a2c8d02" :answer-field/key "$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470575} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32151f04-c89d-5829-9eae-4bce18a92777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668753} {:answer/id #uuid "a480678f-64b9-56af-b3ce-ed45592711a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32151f04-c89d-5829-9eae-4bce18a92777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668753} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef3689c9-19df-5a55-9a13-5076c8b0cb97" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23af5cf8-2a07-56b8-9952-fd82e54a4b1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98646" :question/problem "![](images/54/54cfcbaa5ce20c1881f35e83bb0ec28fa1399cc5100ee745941ff7bb52ac7705.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)^{2}(y-1)$ does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y+2)(y-1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y+2)(y-1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)>0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+2)(y-1)$ does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2},$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-2,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $(y-1)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-2,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}$ has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-(y+2)(y-1)^{2}$ only."}]
